// `item`, `room`, `world`, `test`, `ok`, `err` and `some` introduce declarations
// or patterns in the positions that need them, and are ordinary names everywhere
// else. Each of these used to parse as no expression at all, so the parameter
// read back as `none` and the program answered with a wrong number in silence.
◆ twice(item) ⟦ ^ item * 2 ⟧
◆ pick(room, world) ⟦ ^ room + world ⟧

sums ← [1, 2, 3] → map { |test| test + 10 }

twice(21) + pick(1, 2) + (sums → sum)
